\section{Matching options}{

The call is \verb{MatchIt::matchit(formula, data, method = "nearest", estimand = "ATT", distance = <logit column>, replace = replace, ratio = 1, m.order = m_order, caliper = caliper, std.caliper = TRUE, normalize = FALSE)}. The order \code{"largest"} is
MatchIt's default for the ATT when a numeric distance is supplied: the treated patient with
the largest score is matched first. A caliper is given in standard deviations of the logit
(\code{std.caliper = TRUE}); it drops treated patients, so the share of treated kept is reported.
}

\section{Weights}{

\code{normalize = FALSE} is passed so that MatchIt's weights are the raw matching weights: every
matched treated patient has weight 1, every matched untreated patient has weight equal to
the number of pairs it belongs to, and unmatched patients have weight 0. The sum of the
weights of the untreated patients is therefore the number of matched treated patients, also
with replacement. (MatchIt's default \code{normalize = TRUE} rescales those weights to a mean of
1, which changes no weighted mean or balance statistic but would make that sum equal the
number of distinct untreated partners.) Each row of a match table carries
\code{weight = 1 / ratio}, which is 1 in this version.
}

\section{Bootstrap block}{

With \code{bootstrap_samples = B > 0} the function draws \code{B} nonparametric row bootstrap samples of
the data (one derived seed per sample). A sample with fewer than 10 treated or 10 untreated rows
is skipped, listed in \code{bootstrap$skipped} and counted in a message. For each sample, logistic
regression with the model set's formula (as written, with any interactions or
transformations) is fitted in-sample, without cross-fitting: this block measures the sampling
variability of the fitted model, not its out-of-sample accuracy (see
\code{\link[=bootstrap_rank_instability]{bootstrap_rank_instability()}}). The refitted model predicts logits for every
ORIGINAL patient; their percentile ranks form \code{bootstrap$rank} (n x B). These logits are not
clipped by default, while the cross-fitted scores are clipped to \verb{[clip, 1 - clip]}; under
near-separation many cross-fitted scores can then share the clipped value (tied ranks) while
their bootstrap ranks stay distinct. \code{clip_bootstrap = TRUE} clips the bootstrap scores in the
same way. The default (\code{FALSE}) keeps the method as it was studied. When a factor level
of the original data is absent from the sample, the refit uses the full-data design matrix
and scores those patients as the reference level (the rule of the cross-fitted logistic
model); the samples concerned are listed in
\code{bootstrap$design_rule_samples}. When every level is present but prediction from the
refitted model fails (a rank-deficient fit that did not converge), the refit uses the same
design-matrix rule; those samples are listed in \code{bootstrap$prediction_fallback_samples}.
No sample is skipped for this reason. The sample is then
matched with the same options, rows are mapped back to original ids, and for every
original treated patient present in the sample the untreated partner matched to its first
occurrence
is stored in \code{bootstrap$pairs} (n x B, \code{NA} when absent or unmatched). \code{bootstrap$present}
(n x B, logical) says whether each original patient is in each sample. A skipped sample
keeps its column, filled with \code{NA}, so that every matrix is n x B.
}

\section{Seeds and parallel runs}{

Per-task seeds are derived in the main process from \code{seed}, passed to each worker and
applied with \code{\link[withr:with_seed]{withr::with_seed()}}; \code{m_order = "random"} and the bootstrap draws are
